      "point": "Both identify Huguenots as French Protestants of the Calvinist/Reformed tradition.",
      "quote_britannica": "HUGUENOTS, an application given by way of concept to the referred or Protestant Calvinists of France.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The Huguenots are a religious group of French Protestants who held to the Reformed (Calvinist) tradition of Protestantism."
    },
    {
      "point": "Both associate the origin of the name with events around 1560, including the Amboise plot.",
      "quote_britannica": "The name had its first rife in 1560; but authors are",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The label Huguenot was purportedly first applied in France to those conspirators (all of them aristocratic members of the Reformed Church) who were involved in the Amboise plot of 1560"
    },
    {
      "point": "Both state that the name was originally used in derision or hatred.",
      "quote_britannica": "an application given by way of concept to the referred or Protestant Calvinists of France.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The name, Huguenot, \"the people applied in hatred and derision to those who were elsewhere called Lutherans, and from Touraine it spread throughout France.\""
    },
    {
      "point": "Both note that the etymology of the name is uncertain and debated by authors.",
      "quote_britannica": "The name had its first rife in 1560; but authors are",
      "quote_wikipedia": "A term used originally in derision, Huguenot has unclear origins. Various hypotheses have been promoted."
    }
  ],
  "differences": [
    {
      "point": "Britannica gives only a brief definition and origin date, while Wikipedia provides a detailed history of persecution, wars, and decline.",
      "quote_britannica": "HUGUENOTS, an application given by way of concept to the referred or Protestant Calvinists of France. The name had its first rife in 1560; but authors are",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The Huguenots were concentrated in the southern and western parts of the Kingdom of France. As Huguenots gained influence and more openly displayed their faith, Catholic hostility grew. A series of religious conflicts followed, known as the French Wars of Religion, fought intermittently from 1562 to 1598."
    },
    {
      "point": "Britannica does not mention the Edict of Nantes or its revocation, while Wikipedia treats them as central events.",
      "quote_britannica": "HUGUENOTS, an application given by way of concept to the referred or Protestant Calvinists of France.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The wars ended with the Edict of Nantes of 1598, which granted the Huguenots substantial religious, political and military autonomy."
    },
    {
      "point": "Britannica does not discuss etymological hypotheses, while Wikipedia offers multiple theories about the name's origin.",
      "quote_britannica": "The name had its first rife in 1560; but authors are",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The term may have been a combined reference to the Swiss politician Besançon Hugues (died 1532) and the religiously conflicted nature of Swiss republicanism in his time."
    },
